From dd4dcc9747dae39a79b3de106ed147c37ee1d8ce Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Knifethrower <andersenpeters@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 14:38:05 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 31/34] render: spill the block sprite pixels once a renderer
 has them

RenderAssets kept the block sprite arrays' top level (every layer's RGBA,
20 MiB at the 64 px cells of the default resources) for the whole session,
though nothing reads it after a renderer has uploaded the arrays, until
another renderer is built (another graphics API, a splitscreen player).

Spill it as the GUI atlas's pixels already are: once a renderer is built, a
short-lived thread writes the bytes to cache/sprite-pixels-<size>-<layers>l-
<content key>.rgba and drops the CPU copy; a later build reads them back.
The name is the content's key (astc::content_key), so a launch with the
same textures finds its file and writes nothing, and no other process's
file is ever taken for these bytes; the file is written aside and renamed.
Spills of other textures in that folder go. If the file cannot be written,
the bytes stay in memory as before.

Measured on PC with the R36S's MemTotal (render distance 12, 4 min in a
world, heaptrack on a system-allocator build): the 20.2 MiB block is gone
from the live set. A second launch kept the file; a splitscreen join
(HORIZON_SPLIT=2, Vulkan) built both players' renderers from it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
